    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that looks good. can u expand on the staging/production gates a bit more? specifically i want to focus on what we do when stuff fails those gates—like how we handle unexpected errors or things that bypass the checks to make it into production. we need to be really strict about how we catch those issues.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a gate fails in staging or production, the process should be immediate and decisive, ideally triggering an automated rollback or halting the pipeline entirely. If a test fails, it should be treated as a critical failure, requiring the developer to fix the issue and re-run the pipeline from the beginning—no exceptions. To prevent things from bypassing checks, you must implement immutable infrastructure principles and strictly control access, ensuring that only the automated pipeline can push to production, not individual developers. This strictness is vital; any deviation from the defined path is a security and quality risk.",
      "source": "target",
